The one mutation that was NOT caught (this is the FAIL):

The dispatch brief for this check explicitly named this attack and said a
green suite after it "is a FAIL finding": "make the loader div render with
the wrong hash". I reproduced it:

  In internal/handlers/whatif/handlers.go, analysisFastOrCached's return:
    -	return a, depHash, nil
    +	return a, depHash + "x", nil

  This corrupts the value that flows straight into the loader div's
  `hx-get="/whatif/results-full?hash={{.AsyncHash}}"` — i.e. exactly the
  scenario "the loader div renders with the wrong hash". A wrong hash here
  means the async loader's request would eternally 204 in production (never
  matches settings' real dep-hash), leaving the pending skeleton stuck
  forever — a silent, total failure of the feature's core mechanism.

  Ran: go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... (full package, 9.9s)
  Result: ok — the ENTIRE package suite passed, including
  TestCalculateCacheMissRendersFastWithLoader (which only asserts the body
  `strings.Contains(body, "/whatif/results-full?hash=")` — a prefix check
  that is satisfied even by a corrupted or empty hash value) and
  TestPollCacheMissRendersPending (only checks `id="whatif-async-loader"` is
  present, not what hash it carries).

  No test in results_full_test.go, singleflight_test.go, or handlers_test.go
  extracts the rendered hash from a pending body and round-trips it through
  GET /whatif/results-full to confirm it is the SAME value analysisFastOrCached
  used to build the fast analysis (e.g. asserting the loader's `hash=` query
  param equals the depHash computed independently via buildEngineInput on the
  same settings). All existing tests either supply their own hard-coded hash
  directly to handleWhatIfResultsFull (bypassing the loader's rendered value
  entirely) or only check for the presence of the `hash=` substring.

  I verified the actual shipped code is currently correct (the unmutated
  diff literally is `return a, depHash, nil`), so there is no live user-facing
  bug today. But per this check's explicit mandate ("Test discrimination: ...
  a green suite after such a mutation is a FAIL finding") and per the
  constitution's instruction to default to FAIL on ambiguous evidence and
  report defects the confirming read would miss, I'm failing this: the
  acceptance-criteria-guarding test suite does not actually pin down the one
  correctness property ("the loader fetches the SAME settings it was
  rendered from") that the entire fast-first mechanism depends on, and this
  is easy to fix (assert the hash substring after `?hash=` equals the
  independently-computed buildEngineInput hash, in
  TestCalculateCacheMissRendersFastWithLoader and
  TestPollCacheMissRendersPending).

Mutation restored afterward; working tree in the scratch copy verified clean
via `diff` against the pre-mutation backup before this verdict was written.
